+# Feature Specification: JSON Patch
+
+**Standard**: RFC 6902 (JSON Patch) §3 (document structure), §4 (operations: 4.1 add, 4.2 remove, 4.3 replace,
+4.4 move, 4.5 copy, 4.6 test), §5 (error handling); paths are RFC 6901 JSON Pointers (§3, §4). Documents are
+RFC 8259 JSON values.
+
+## User Story 1 - Apply a patch (P1)
+A service calls `applyPatch(document, patch)` and receives the patched document as a new value.
+
+Acceptance (RFC 6902 Appendix A examples A.1–A.16 all produce the documented results), including:
+- add: object member (adds or replaces), array element (inserts, shifting later ones), `-` (appends),
+  index equal to length (appends), path `""` (replaces the whole document).
+- remove: object member or array element (later elements shift down); removing the root (`""`) is an error
+  because the result would not be a JSON value.
+- replace: target must exist; value replaced; path `""` replaces the whole document.
+- move: `from` must exist; equivalent to remove at `from` then add at `path` with the removed value.
+  `from` equal to `path` is a no-op. `from` a proper prefix (strict ancestor) of `path` is an error (§4.4).
+- copy: `from` must exist; the value is deep-copied (later changes to one do not affect the other).
+- test: succeeds when the value at `path` equals `value` (§4.6: same type; strings equal by code points;
+  numbers numerically; arrays same length and elementwise; objects same member names and equal values,
+  order irrelevant; `null` equals only `null`). Otherwise it fails.
+- Operations apply sequentially; each sees the result of the previous one. Empty patch returns an equal copy.
+
+## User Story 2 - Reject bad patches and failed operations (P1)
+Throws a typed `JsonPatchError` when:
+- the patch is not an array, an operation is not an object, `op` is missing or not one of the six names
+  (§4), `path` is missing or not a string, `from` is missing/not a string for move/copy, `value` is
+  absent for add/replace/test (`value: null` is valid) (§4);
+- a path is not a valid JSON Pointer (RFC 6901 §3);
+- the parent of an add target does not exist, or the target of remove/replace/move-from/copy-from/test does
+  not exist, an array index is out of range, invalid (leading zeros, non-digits) or `-` where an existing
+  element is required (§4.1–4.6);
+- a traversal enters a primitive/`null`;
+- a test operation's values are not equal (§4.6, §5).
+
+## Edge cases
+- Atomicity (§5): if any operation fails, the whole patch fails and nothing is partially returned.
+- The input document and patch are never mutated; the result shares no mutable structure with the input
+  document or with the patch's `value`s (Constitution IV).
+- `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` as member names are ordinary own keys: they never touch the
+  prototype chain, neither as a path token nor as keys of cloned/added data.
+- Members present in an operation object other than the ones defined are ignored (§4).
+- Duplicate-key handling is out of scope.
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 `applyPatch(document: unknown, patch: unknown): unknown` exported from `src/index.ts`, documented.
+- FR-2 Failures throw `JsonPatchError` (extends `Error`), exported.
+- FR-3 No runtime dependencies; additive API change (minor).
+
+## Assumptions
+- Root removal: an error, since the result would not be a JSON value.
+- A patch `value` that is not JSON (e.g. `undefined` as property value) counts as absent.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- All RFC 6902 Appendix A examples pass; every error condition above is tested; `npm test` is clean.
